text:
toni: quick heads-up: the new edge sample (vite + esbuild target) is showing weird TS inference for our SDK generics. users see the generic inferred as unknown/any and overload resolution is different between node dev and the built edge bundle.

yoel: can you paste the minimal repro?

toni: sure. simplified sdk code:

```ts
// src/client.ts (published package)
export function fetchThing(id: string): Promise<{ id: string; ok: true }>;
export function fetchThing<T>(id: string, opts?: { parseAs: (raw: unknown) => T }): Promise<T>;
export function fetchThing(id: string, opts?: any) {
  // runtime implementation
  return Promise.resolve(opts?.parseAs ? opts.parseAs({ id }) : ({ id, ok: true }));
}
```

// consumer edge app
```ts
import { fetchThing } from 'redwood-sdk';

const thing = await fetchThing('123'); // in node-ts -> inferred as {id: string; ok: true}
const thing2 = await fetchThing('123', { parseAs: raw => raw as { id: string; value: number } });
```

toni: locally (tsserver) the intellisense shows correct overloads. after vite build + esbuild target=es2020, users open the built source and the types appear to point at the wrong d.ts (some bundlers pick up dist/cjs/index.d.ts).

rachel: likely moduleResolution hitting node16 vs bundler. Vite uses esbuild for transforms but tsc resolution during type checking in the consumer can differ. Check package.json exports + types map.

omar: we solved a similar case by publishing a per-subpath types file. i.e. in package.json: 

```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
    "types": "./dist/types/index.d.ts"
  },
  "./client": {
    "import": "./dist/esm/client.js",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/client.js",
    "types": "./dist/types/client.d.ts"
  }
}
```

omar: that way the consumer tooling more reliably picks the matching d.ts for the ESM entry.

yoel: also add a small `typesVersions` block to help older TypeScript pick the right typings for different TS versions. we've used: 

```json
"typesVersions": { ">=4.5": { "*": ["dist/types/*"] } }
```

toni: good call. one follow-up: should we stop using overloads on public functions (they break often), and instead provide a single generic + helper overloads internally?

sam: i recommend a single generic signature for the exported surface. Overloads are fragile across declaration merging and bundling. For example: 

```ts
export function fetchThing<T = {id: string; ok: true}>(id: string, opts?: { parseAs?: (raw: unknown) => T }): Promise<T> {
  // impl
}
```

sam: keep overloads only in internal files where you control consumption. Exports should be resilient.

rachel: minor DX thing: include a `tsconfig.edge.json` in the sample repo that sets "moduleResolution": "bundler" and "module": "ESNext". Add a README note: "If your project is vite/rollup/webpack 5, prefer moduleResolution=bundler."

omar: for Vite specifically, add an esbuild plugin option: 

```js
esbuild: {
  target: 'es2020',
  keepNames: true
}
```

omar: keepNames helps when minifiers collapse helper names used in declaration-only code paths.

yoel: another gotcha: declarationMap + sourceMap can confuse editors into surfacing the wrong d.ts. we disabled declarationMap in our package to reduce editor surprises.

toni: interesting. are there runtime risks or just typing ones? user reported a runtime error when they passed a lambda parseAs that later got stripped by the bundler due to tree-shaking.

sam: if parseAs is passed inline it shouldn't be stripped; more likely the consumer's bundler rewired the import to a different entry missing the exported symbol. conditional exports again.

rachel: suggestion checklist for PR:
- make package ESM-first with explicit exports mapping for each subpath
- ship per-entry types in dist/types/* and add typesVersions
- prefer single generic exported signatures, avoid public overloads
- add tsconfig.edge.json to sample and a tiny script: `npm run build:edge` that runs vite/esbuild with the same flags as the deploy target
- turn off declarationMap in published dts to avoid editor confusion

omar: i'll slip a tiny esbuild call into the sample test that builds the consumer and runs tsc --noEmit against the built tree so we catch type drift.

toni: perfect. i'll open the PR with changes and link the sample. thanks all. :pray:

doc-bot: created issue #482: "edge sample: ESM typing drift and overload surprises" (assigned to toni).

sam: one last note: document the `unknown` boundary pattern for parse helpers. e.g. `parseAs: (raw: unknown) => T` and in SDK internals accept `unknown` from network. that forces consumers to be explicit.

toni: love it. pairing tomorrow at 10 to land the changes.
